[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  friday september 9th i'm zoe thomas for  the wall street journal  so many of the products and services we  buy today rely on tech running behind  the scenes to deliver them faster more  efficiently or directly to us skipping  the middleman  forerunner ventures is well aware of  this the 10 year old venture capital  firm concentrates on consumer-focused  tech startups it's invested in companies  like fintech firm chime health tech  brand aura and gamer platform medify  but now amid an economic downturn that  follows the rush of vc funding that  poured into startups over the last few  years  how are vcs thinking about what to  invest in next  and how can they help the startups  they've already backed  to discuss this and more we're joined by  kirsten greene the founder and managing  partner of 4runner ventures hi kirsten  thanks for joining us thank you so much  zoe for having me on today  so forerunner typically invests in  consumer focused startups given  inflation given the kind of economic  uncertainty we have at the moment what  kinds of things are you focused on now  you know i think one of the things about  investing in venture capital is you  really do need to take a long view of  the market  our job is to think about what is going  to be incredibly relevant a couple of  years out but has enough relevancy today  that there's an early audience for it  and to get traction  so we think very much less about what's  happening in the moment in terms of the  economic cycles and more about where's  the consumer at what does that maybe  imply about the need or the opportunity  going forward and investing into that  you know we've heard from some other vcs  that now is the time to batten down the  hatches there's been some real warning  messages coming out from other investors  saying you know startups need to cut  back after years of this big boom this  spending spree hiring sprees  it sounds like you're not really in that  camp though we are in that camp but i  don't think that's just blanket advice  for everybody to say  you know the world has changed stop  spending start cutting  i think coming off of the last two years  where we've been in a time period of  tremendous growth  which has meant  tremendous growth in team sizes  tremendous growth in marketing budgets  tremendous mandates to grow in general  as companies like that sort of requires  most everybody to take a hard look at  those elements of their business and say  do we really need  all of the spend that we've accumulated  in the last two years if we're in a  period where it's not just growth growth  growth it's  more holistic about all elements of your  business  and with an asterisk around you've got  to figure out how to  have some opportunity to control your  own destiny you know there's  been a number of layoffs announced i  know people are looking you know closely  again at their cost structures decisions  about people are really hard to make  they're personal and they also you know  any team that grew quickly or hired a  lot of people or any people spent a lot  of time doing that so these are you know  they feel like  challenging decisions to make when you  bring the human element into it but in  so many ways it's you know required  right now as you think about how to  again create the flexibility and the  nimbleness in your business  are there areas that you've seen  startups may be more willing to  negotiate on than they were over the  past few months or a few years you know  it's interesting right now because i  think what hasn't been as clear this  year is  that question around  you know  how much  negotiating room either side has on on  deals and i think a lot of that has to  do with the fact that  companies raised a considerable amount  of money over the last two years and so  there's been less businesses that i  think have been in a situation where  they need to raise capital they've  decided this is the right moment to  raise capital  and i feel like that combination is  really going to extend this question  into 2023.  i think there's going to be more  companies in the market looking for  follow-on dollars in 23 than there were  in 22  and those are really the opportunities  that will create the data points to  understand  how valuations have have changed  so what would you want to get from  companies that are looking for funding  in 2023 i mean what kind of things are  you looking to see and to get from them  in return for your capital  the focus at least on our team i think  we've always focused on unit economics  even very early  different environments require you to  have more evidence of that than others  so even if you have an idea of like okay  here's the business model we kind of  understand how this is going to play out  we can take some leaps and assuming on  how it's going to play out i think  people are less willing to take those  leaps and they want to see more proof  points  and part of that is because i think that  is not just the uncertainty in the  market that maybe tests the belief but  it's just seeing how founders operate  like are they  aware of the environment around them and  balancing the right set of elements and  does that build conviction in their  ability to grow a business in a state of  uncertainty because nobody knows like  how long this period is going to last or  whatever but like the real opportunity  for everybody right now is to kind of  demonstrate your execution capabilities  and the real potential of your business  based on fundamentals  all right i want to end by talking about  a space that i've heard you're a  believer in the metaverse can you tell  us a bit about that maybe some of the  investments that you're making any  opportunities that you see here  our north star in investing is the  consumer we really think about  what's going on with people how are  their lives changing how are their needs  changing and then where can business  solve those and address those the  metaverse is  curious to us and interesting it's  another dimension it's another way for  people to engage and interact  i think we've been  you know impressed or at least inspired  by  many different potential applications  that said it doesn't yet seamlessly  integrate into consumer experience or  people's lives  and i think that we really need to find  a bridge  to bring it into the broader mainstream  can you explain that idea a bit more  that bridge to consumers what do you  mean by that for instance like last year  there was so much conversation and so  much enthusiasm over nfts you know it  captured entrepreneurs imagination it  captured investors imagination and with  the billions of dollars of transactions  that happened on platforms like openc  and others it clearly captured some  people in the markets attention  i think though that there still remains  a big question on like okay what is it  what do i do with this what's the useful  application of this  and i think that  the bridge could be in unlocking  something like an nft  that is less about an advanced jpeg and  more about  a tool for access to  a loyalty program or to  your you know club membership that you  bought or to you know different version  of like executing a subscription model  something that is sort of like we're  doing elements of this today in other  ways in a business  what blockchain or the technology that  these nfts sit on for example offers is  a better way to execute those  experiences with a twist that unlocks  more potential going forward  all right kirsten green from 4runner  ventures thank you so much for joining  us zoe thanks so much for your  thoughtful questions  and that's it for tech news briefing  this week our producer is julie chang  our supervising producer is chris  sinsley our executive producer is kateri  yokum and i'm your host zoe thomas  thanks for listening and have a great  weekend  [Music]  you

